        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Iolite is the gem variety of cordierite, a magnesium, iron and aluminium silicate. It forms in clay-rich rocks metamorphosed at high temperature and low to moderate pressure: hornfels at granite contacts, gneisses and granulites. It also occurs in granites and pegmatites, and concentrates in alluvial deposits. Its violetish-blue colour comes from iron, distributed between two sites in its structure. It is sometimes called “water sapphire” or, in the past, “dichroite”, because of its spectacular pleochroism.",
            "Orthorhombic system. Short prisms, often twinned into pseudo-hexagons; rolled grains. Poor cleavage; conchoidal to uneven fracture. Hardness 7 to 7.5; specific gravity 2.57 to 2.66; vitreous lustre; refractive index 1.53 to 1.57; birefringence 0.008 to 0.012 (biaxial). Very strong pleochroism, its key feature: violetish blue, pale blue and straw yellow to colourless depending on the viewing axis; the cutter must orient the table to show the blue. Oriented haematite inclusions give “bloodshot” iolite. Rarely treated, it wears well daily if knocks are avoided.",
